WEBVTT

00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:06.242
Three tricky moments in the rhythm conversation.

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:10.842
Moment one: the meeting nobody likes, but nobody can drop.

00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:16.662
Usually it’s one that someone outside the team expects, like a status report for a steering group.

00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:18.582
Don’t pretend you can drop it.

00:00:18.632 --> 00:00:22.102
Ask instead: “How can we make it cost us less?”

00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:25.762
Often it can become a written update, shared once a week.

00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.382
Moment two: different people need different rhythms.

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:33.242
Some love daily check-ins, others find them a waste of time.

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:38.582
Some need long stretches of focus, others work in short bursts. That’s normal.

00:00:38.632 --> 00:00:42.262
Look for a minimum shared rhythm, and let the rest be flexible.

00:00:42.312 --> 00:00:46.082
You can use what you learned in the “Who are we?” conversation here.

00:00:46.132 --> 00:00:49.222
Moment three: the review doesn’t happen.

00:00:49.272 --> 00:00:51.442
This is the most common outcome.

00:00:51.492 --> 00:00:58.802
Teams agree to a review moment, and after two weeks it’s the first meeting to be cancelled when things get busy. Protect it.

00:00:58.852 --> 00:01:05.582
Put it in the calendar right away, keep it short, and make it concrete: look at real work, not slides.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:09.142
One more tip: be careful with the “remove” pile.

00:01:09.192 --> 00:01:11.602
Removing meetings feels great in the room.

00:01:11.652 --> 00:01:17.362
But if a meeting was the only place where some people got information, removing it can leave them out.

00:01:17.412 --> 00:01:23.282
For every meeting you drop, ask: “Who might miss something, and how will they hear about it now?”

00:01:23.332 --> 00:01:25.922
And after one cycle, come back to it.

00:01:25.972 --> 00:01:29.628
Ten minutes is enough: what worked, what didn’t, what do we change?

